`{import_name}.testing` provides `FakeCountdown`, a stand-in for `Countdown` whose fire state is controlled directly by the test, so downstream code that owns a `Countdown` can be driven through deadlines without managing real ticks.  The module declares `__chumicro_test_support__ = True`, so the deploy filter keeps it on the host and it never lands on a device.

## Usage

```python
from {import_name}.testing import FakeCountdown

def test_screensaver_dims_on_idle_timeout():
    idle = FakeCountdown()
    screen = Screensaver(timeout=idle)

    idle.set_fired(False)
    screen.tick()
    assert not screen.dimmed

    idle.set_fired(True)
    screen.tick()
    assert screen.dimmed
```

`FakeCountdown` defaults to "not fired" so a fresh instance behaves like an armed countdown that hasn't elapsed.  Call `set_fired(True)` to simulate the deadline passing.

## Test hooks

| Hook | What it does |
|---|---|
| `set_fired(True)` | Next `fired(now_ms)` returns `True` once, then `False` thereafter, matching the real `Countdown`'s one-shot semantics. |
| `set_fired(False)` | Reset the fake to the armed state. |
| `reset_calls` | List of `(now_ms,)` tuples recorded each time the code under test called `reset()`.  Assert on this to verify reset ordering. |

## Using these fakes in your own tests

Install `{distribution}` and import the fakes straight into your test suite:

```python
from {import_name}.testing import FakeCountdown
```

Project convention: libraries that expose injectable services ship their own test fakes alongside the production code.

